<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Mesoamerica, Olmecs to Aztecs</h1><p class="path-tagline">From the Olmec heartland to Tenochtitlan's fall: the cities and states of Mexico and Central America.</p><p class="path-intro">From about 1200 BCE, when the Olmecs built San Lorenzo on Mexico's Gulf Coast, to the fall of Tenochtitlan in 1521, the peoples of Mesoamerica raised cities, carved monuments to their rulers and fought for tribute. Olmec images of rulers and gods helped shape the art of later peoples such as the Maya, and Teotihuacan's influence reached as far as the Maya cities of Tikal and Copán. The Maya never formed a single state, and after the southern kingdoms emptied, Chichén Itzá led the north, while the Aztecs looked back to the Toltecs of Tula. The last stops show the Aztec Triple Alliance and two neighbours that held out against it, the Purépecha and Tlaxcala.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/olmecs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 1200 BCE – c. 400 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Olmecs</span><span class="path-step-why">The Olmecs built the first great centres of Mesoamerica, San Lorenzo and then La Venta, in the lowlands of Mexico's Gulf Coast between about 1200 and 400 BCE.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Olmecs were the earliest major civilisation of Mesoamerica, flourishing in the Gulf Coast lowlands of southern Veracruz and Tabasco in Mexico from about 1200 to 400 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Olmec colossal heads</span><span class="path-step-why">Seventeen basalt heads up to 3.4 metres high, thought to be portraits of rulers, carved from stone brought from the Sierra de los Tuxtlas by means still unknown.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Olmec colossal heads are seventeen heads carved from basalt boulders by the Olmec civilisation of Mexico's Gulf Coast: ten from San Lorenzo, four from La Venta, two from Tres Zapotes and one from La Cobata.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/maya-civilisation"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-inscriptions.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 2000 BCE – 1697 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Maya civilisation</span><span class="path-step-why">The Maya built hundreds of cities but never a single state, and their scribes created the most complete writing system of the ancient Americas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/teotihuacan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 100 BCE – 550 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Teotihuacan</span><span class="path-step-why">A planned city of perhaps 100,000 people in the Valley of Mexico, whose pyramids and architecture influenced Mesoamerica as far as the Maya cities of Tikal and Copán.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/palenque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palenque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Place · c. 226 BCE–c. 799</span><span class="path-step-name">Palenque</span><span class="path-step-why">Sacked by Calakmul in 599 and 611, this Maya city was rebuilt under K'inich Janaab' Pakal, whose tomb was found in the Temple of the Inscriptions in 1952.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Palenque, anciently known as Lakamha, is the site of a Maya city-state near the Usumacinta River in the Mexican state of Chiapas; its ruins date from about 226 BCE to about 799 CE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chichen-itza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Place · c. 600–1100</span><span class="path-step-name">Chichen Itza</span><span class="path-step-why">In the 10th century this Yucatán city rose to be a regional capital of the northern Maya, its buildings mixing central Mexican styles with those of the region.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Chichen Itza was a large pre-Columbian city built by the Maya people, whose ruins lie in Yucatán, Mexico, between Valladolid and Mérida.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/toltec-state"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 900 CE – 1150 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Toltec state</span><span class="path-step-why">Tula's warrior statues and colonnaded halls have close parallels at Chichén Itzá, and the Aztecs later revered the Toltecs as the founders of civilisation.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/aztec-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/templo-mayor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1325 CE – 1521 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Aztec Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From 1428 the Triple Alliance of Tenochtitlan, Texcoco and Tlacopan ruled central Mexico through tribute, from an island city of 200,000 or more people.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Aztec Empire was the Triple Alliance of Tenochtitlan, Texcoco and Tlacopan, formed in 1428 in the Valley of Mexico and dominated by the Mexica of Tenochtitlan.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/purepecha-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/curicaueri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1300 CE – 1530 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Purépecha Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Ruled from Tzintzuntzan on Lake Pátzcuaro, this western kingdom routed Axayacatl's Aztec army in the late 1470s and led Mesoamerica in copper and bronze.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Purépecha Empire, also called the Tarascan state, was a powerful polity in western Mexico centred on Lake Pátzcuaro.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/tlaxcala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maxixcatzin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1348 CE – 1521 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Tlaxcala</span><span class="path-step-why">Four Nahua city-states that resisted the Triple Alliance for generations, then allied with Hernán Cortés in 1519 and helped take Tenochtitlan in 1521.</span></span></a></li></ol>
